9. VECTOR LANGUAGE CREATION MUST USE THE ACTUAL NETWORK

The Vector Language must support actual entity creation.

Spaces

A valid program must be able to request creation of a Space through the Network protocol.

The Space must be committed only after Network-level authorization and consensus.

Regions

A valid program must be able to request creation of a Region under an existing verified Space.

The Region must be committed under the parent Space’s authorization and validator scope.

Wallets

A valid program must be able to request creation of a Wallet under an existing verified Region.

Wallet participant registrations and key associations must use the independent authorization protocol.

Tokens

A valid program must be able to create a Token under an existing verified Wallet.

Token creation must enforce supply and vector rules.

Ghost entities

If the language exposes Ghost Region, Ghost Wallet or Ghost Token creation, it must route through Network-level authorization and verification.

vContracts

A valid program must be able to define and create a vContract that becomes a real protocol object.

Its executable rules must be deterministic and stored in the verified event history.

vLoans

A valid program must be able to define and create a vLoan using the actual vLoan protocol implementation.

Loan principal, collateral and repayment operations must follow the existing Token and authorization invariants.

Transfers

A valid program must be able to transfer Tokens between eligible entities, with real source and destination authorization, correct routing, applicable drain rules and final consensus.

Never reduce Vector Language to a text editor that merely generates API request strings.

It is the human-readable programming interface to the actual Rust protocol.

